1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Okay, quick question before we even start.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:09,000
In the last 24 hours, how many times do you
think you used artificial intelligence?

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,500
Take a guess. Five? Maybe ten?

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,500
The real answer is probably closer to a hundred.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:20,500
And I'm going to prove that to you
by the end of this video.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:26,000
But here's the strange thing — almost nobody
who uses AI a hundred times a day can explain it.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Ten minutes from now, you'll never freeze
on that question again.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Let me start with something you've
literally seen this week.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,500
You open Swiggy, place an order, and the
screen says — arriving in 32 minutes.

10
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,500
Most of us close the app and move on.
But stop and look at that number.

11
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,500
It's stranger than it looks.

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,500
First — it's not a fixed timer.

13
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:00,500
If you'd ordered the same biryani an hour later,
it might've said 41 minutes.

14
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:05,000
Something is changing the number
based on the moment.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Second — and this is the part nobody thinks
about — there is no human doing this.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,500
Nobody at Swiggy is sitting in a control room
typing "32" for every single order.

17
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,500
At the scale of millions of orders an hour,
that's just impossible.

18
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,000
So something else is making this guess.

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Not a person. Not a fixed timer. Something new.
And that's the whole story of AI.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Now let's try to build this ourselves.

21
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
If somebody handed you the job, you'd
probably think like a normal programmer.

22
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,500
You'd write rules.

23
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,500
Restaurant is busy? Add five minutes.

24
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:51,500
It's raining? Add seven — nobody likes
riding a scooter in Bombay rain.

25
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:56,000
Driver is more than two kilometres away?
Add four for traffic.

26
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Weekend? Add three.

27
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And honestly, this feels great.
For the first twenty orders, it actually works.

28
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
This is exactly how software has been
written for fifty years.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,500
A human sits down, thinks of every situation,
and writes a rule for it.

30
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,500
You are the brain. The computer just
follows instructions.

31
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:25,000
And then reality walks in and slaps your
little program in the face.

32
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,500
Because what happens when it's a rainy
Saturday, during lunch rush —

33
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:35,000
and it's a festival, and the driver is
carrying two other orders —

34
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,500
and the restaurant is slow today specifically?

35
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:44,000
The combinations explode into
millions of possibilities.

36
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And every time you patch one situation,
two new ones break.

37
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,500
I've seen developers lose weeks to this.
Thousands of rules contradicting each other.

38
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:58,500
And the app still gets it wrong.

39
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:03,000
And on top of that, reality keeps changing.

40
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
New restaurants, new areas, festivals,
road closures —

41
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,000
faster than any human could ever sit
down and write rules for.

42
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,500
That's the wall. That's the moment smart
engineers stopped and said:

43
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:23,500
"wait — what if we're doing this
completely backwards?"

44
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:30,000
No human can write all the rules.
Reality is just too messy.

45
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
So here's the flip. One of the most
beautiful ideas in all of computing.

46
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
In the old way, a human writes the rules.
In the new way, the computer discovers them.

47
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
In the old way, you tell the computer what to do.

48
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,500
In the new way, you show it
what already happened.

49
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:55,500
The old way breaks on situations you forgot.

50
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:01,500
The new way actually gets better
the more examples it sees.

51
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:07,000
Think about how a three-year-old
learns what a dog is.

52
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,500
You don't hand the kid a rulebook —
"a dog has four legs, fur, a tail."

53
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:17,500
You just point. "Dog. Dog. That's a dog too."

54
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:23,000
And after enough examples, the kid just knows.
Even a dog they've never seen before.

55
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Nobody wrote the rule. The pattern got
absorbed from examples.

56
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
That — exactly that — is the shift.
From telling, to showing.

57
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
So how does a computer "learn from examples"
in practice? It's three simple steps.

58
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,500
Don't worry, none of them are scary.

59
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:50,500
Step one — Data. You gather a giant
pile of past examples.

60
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:55,000
For Swiggy, that's every order
they've ever delivered.

61
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Step two — Pattern. The computer studies
the pile and finds the hidden connections.

62
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,500
This bundle of patterns it noticed
has a name. We call it a model.

63
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:11,500
Step three — Predict. Your new order goes in,
and out comes a prediction.

64
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:14,000
Thirty-two minutes.

65
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,500
Data, pattern, predict. Remember those three
words and you understand every AI system on Earth.

66
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:26,500
Now let's slow down and look at each
step with our Swiggy example.

67
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:32,500
Step one, the data. Picture a giant spreadsheet —
ten million rows long.

68
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:37,500
Every single row is one real delivery
that already happened.

69
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:45,500
Like this one. Domino's, clear evening, short
distance, light traffic, Ravi — 28 minutes.

70
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:53,500
This one. McDonald's, pouring rain, far away,
terrible traffic — 47 minutes.

71
00:05:53,500 --> 00:06:01,500
Behrouz, clear weather, medium distance,
medium traffic — 34 minutes.

72
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:09,500
Burger King, clear, very close, light traffic,
also Ravi — 22 minutes.

73
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:15,000
But here is the column that matters —
this last one. Actual time.

74
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
What actually happened. This is the answer
key at the back of the textbook.

75
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
The computer gets to see the questions
and the answers — millions of times over.

76
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
That's the raw material. Now —
what does it do with all of it?

77
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Step two — and this is the one everyone
thinks is magic. It isn't.

78
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,500
It's just very, very fast noticing.

79
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:46,500
Let me show you what "finding the
pattern" literally means.

80
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:53,000
First, take all those past orders and plot
them on a simple graph —

81
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
distance going across, delivery time going up.

82
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,500
Each dot is one past order.

83
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:08,500
Even just by looking, you can already see
something — farther distances mean longer times.

84
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:13,500
The dots are sloping up to the right.

85
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:19,000
Now here's what the computer does —
it draws the single line that best fits all those dots.

86
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,500
The one that runs through the middle
of the cloud as cleanly as possible.

87
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:29,500
Nobody told it the slope of that line.
It found the slope itself, from the dots.

88
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:35,500
That line, right there — that line is the pattern.

89
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:41,500
And the pattern, this thing the computer
drew, has a name. We call it a model.

90
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:46,500
You're going to hear that word for the
rest of your life now.

91
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:52,500
Every time someone says "a model," picture this:
the line drawn through millions of past examples.

92
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:58,500
Step three. The payoff. And this is
where it gets beautifully simple.

93
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:04,000
We already have the model — the line, right there.

94
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
We don't have to do anything fancy.
We just have to read off the answer.

95
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Your new order has a certain distance.
Go up from that point on the bottom axis.

96
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,500
Until you hit the line. That dot —
that's where you land on the pattern.

97
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:23,500
Then slide across to the time axis.
Thirty-two minutes.

98
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:28,500
That's the number on your screen.

99
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:34,000
No rule was written. No human typed a number.

100
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:40,000
The model literally read your answer off the line
it learned from millions of past deliveries.

101
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,000
That is the "something else" we were
hunting for at the very start.

102
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,000
So now — finally — we can say
what AI actually is.

103
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
And it'll mean something now, because you
just watched it happen on screen.

104
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,000
AI is software that learns the pattern
from examples,

105
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
instead of being told the rules.

106
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,500
Read it with me, slowly, because every
word is doing work.

107
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:16,500
Learns — it improves on its own.
We didn't program it step by step.

108
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:22,500
Pattern from examples — it studied
millions of past cases.

109
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:27,500
That was our cloud of dots.

110
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:33,500
Instead of being told the rules — and
that is the whole revolution.

111
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:38,500
No human wrote the logic.
The machine worked it out.

112
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:44,500
That's the difference between knowing
the definition and understanding it.

113
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:50,000
Let me make sure the contrast really lands —
this is the line that separates the two worlds.

114
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:55,500
Normal software is like a calculator.
Two plus two is four. Every single time, forever.

115
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:00,500
A human wrote that rule decades ago
and it never changes.

116
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:05,500
It's predictable. It's exact. It's rigid.

117
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:10,500
AI is the opposite kind of tool.
The more data you feed it, the more accurate it becomes.

118
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:16,000
It actually gets better over time.

119
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,000
It's messy, it's probabilistic,
it sometimes gets things wrong —

120
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,500
but it can handle problems too complicated
for any human to write rules for.

121
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:31,500
Like predicting a biryani's journey
across a rainy city.

122
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:36,500
Neither one is "better." They're different
tools for different jobs.

123
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:42,500
You wouldn't use AI to add two numbers.
And you can't use a calculator to predict traffic.

124
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:48,500
Okay, two words. Tattoo them in your brain —
they're the key to this entire series.

125
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:52,500
Rules...

126
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:56,500
versus patterns.

127
00:10:56,500 --> 00:11:01,500
Old software runs on rules.
Mechanical, written by a human. Rigid.

128
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:08,500
AI runs on patterns — learned from data.
Organic. It bends to fit the shape of reality.

129
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:14,500
Neural networks, ChatGPT, image generators —
all of it sits on the patterns side of this line.

130
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:19,500
So from today, here's your new habit.

131
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:25,000
Every time you hear "AI" out in the wild —
in a headline, from your boss, in an ad —

132
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:31,000
quietly replace it in your head with
"a thing that learned a pattern."

133
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:36,500
You'll be right almost every time.
And you'll understand it better than the person who said it.

134
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:42,000
Now — remember my promise at the very start?
That you use AI close to a hundred times a day?

135
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Let me pay that off. Watch how fast this goes.

136
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Google Maps saying you'll reach by 6:42 —
same trick as Swiggy. Patterns from millions of past trips.

137
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Gmail quietly sending spam to the spam folder —
pattern, not rule.

138
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:04,000
YouTube picking the next video that somehow
keeps you watching for three more hours — pattern.

139
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:09,500
UPI catching a fraudulent payment in the
half-second before your money moves.

140
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:13,500
Instagram's Explore page that knows you a little too well.

141
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:19,000
Your phone camera finding faces in a crowd
and blurring the background in portrait mode.

142
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Every. Single. One. Pattern, not rule.

143
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:30,000
And that's just six — I could do this
for the next twenty minutes.

144
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,500
That's your hundred times a day. You've
been living inside AI this whole time.

145
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:40,000
Today you just learned to see it.

146
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:45,000
So let's lock it in. Four things, and you're done.

147
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:50,500
One: traditional software means
humans write the rules.

148
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:56,500
Two: AI means the computer learns
the pattern from examples.

149
00:12:56,500 --> 00:13:02,000
Three: it happens in three steps —
data, pattern, predict.

150
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Four: that learned pattern is called a model.

151
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:14,500
If those four things make sense, you're already
ahead of most people who work in tech.

152
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:20,000
But — and you might've already felt this itch —
I cheated a little today.

153
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:25,500
All through this video I kept saying this magic
phrase: "the computer finds the pattern."

154
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:30,000
And every time, I just moved on.

155
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:36,500
But how? How does a machine — which is really
just doing math on numbers —

156
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:42,000
actually "draw the right line" through
millions of messy real-world food orders?

157
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:48,000
Is it magic? Is it just averaging?
Is it secretly intelligent?

158
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
That question — how the learning actually
happens — is the real heart of this whole subject.

159
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:00,000
And it's exactly where we go next.

160
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,500
Next time, we untangle the four words everyone
mixes up — AI, ML, Deep Learning, and GenAI.

161
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:13,000
After that episode, you'll be the person at the
table who actually knows the difference.

162
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:18,000
If something clicked today — if AI feels even
slightly less mysterious than it did ten minutes ago —

163
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:23,500
do one thing for me. Send this to that one
friend who keeps asking "yaar, what even is AI?"

164
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:30,000
You'll save them the confusion.
And you'll sound brilliant. See you in the next one.
